3:Revision 3, 2026-11-18, after D52 (changes requested; the owner raised it as D49 after the fourth T03 review). What changed from revision 2: R4 gains the ambiguity policy, and this design now says how the categoriser reaches a verdict on a credit, which rules are protected, and what settles an ambiguous credit. The changes are in Context, two new sub-decisions, C1, the rule tables and line provenance in Data changes, Failure modes, Migration, Observability and Consumer trace items 15 and 16. Every other requirement, property and id is unchanged. The shipped cash limits now carry the owner's D35 answer, which revision 2 still showed as an open question. Revision 2 (2026-11-16, after D34) counted cash deposits as trading receipts and flagged the large ones (D31), showed pass/refer/fail with an illustrative label (D28), kept timing out of the tool (D32) and wrote the pack once, at sign-off. The owner confirmed local-first (D29), OBIE v3.1 and TrueLayer first (D30) and Tier 1 only by 2026-11-29 (D33).
25:- D47: a line whose matching rules span two or more buckets goes to the judgement queue with every candidate recorded.
27:- D52 puts the policy into R4: a credit whose wording is ambiguous or conflicting goes to the judgement queue and is never counted as trading receipts.
69:**Sub-decision: how the categoriser settles wording that points two ways (D52, revision 3).** (i) First match in table order, with exceptions inside rules to steer known collisions: v1, and T03's attempts so far. (ii) Two stages, with disagreement as a verdict of its own: line-type cues decide when any match; names decide only when no cue matches; within the deciding stage, matches in two or more buckets make the credit ambiguous. A match inside a longer listed phrase counts only as the longer one, and the bank's own returned-payment code settles the line type alone. (iii) Any two buckets anywhere, cue or name, make the credit ambiguous. *Chose (ii)*. (i) lost because order is a silent choice. Each review round found a new pair of lists it got wrong, and an exception that hides one list's cue when another's is present (HMRC hidden by savings wording) turns a disagreement into a guess nobody sees. (iii) lost because a cue says what the line is and a name says who sent it. Every refund from PayPal, Amazon or an acquirer, and every bounced direct debit to HMRC, would go to the queue though its wording is not in doubt, and it would undo the owner's D46 rule that the cue wins. What (ii) gives up: a cue in payer-written text outranks names, so a payer who writes REFUND moves their credit to refunds. Every cue is non-trading, so this can only understate trading receipts, never invent them. Which reading of D52 the owner meant is an open question.
71:**Sub-decision: what settles an ambiguous credit (revision 3).** (i) Only a per-line override. (ii) A per-line override, a case-profile name or an own-account pairing; never a firm rule. (iii) Anything that ranks above the built-in table's unprotected rules, firm rules included. *Chose (ii)*. (i) contradicts R5, R6 and R8, which say a credit from a director or own account named in the profile, or a paired transfer, SHALL take that category; the owner asked to keep them. Profile names and pairing only ever give non-trading buckets, so R4's rule that an ambiguous credit is never trading receipts holds. (iii) lost because a firm rule can name trading receipts, and it was previewed on another case's lines, not on this one. It would count ambiguous wording as trading without a person looking at the line.
96:- **C1 Rule tables and categoriser** (src/cma/categorise.py; rule tables as data under src/cma/rules/). Built-in tables v1 (today's, frozen, first match wins) and v2 (the new lists, in two stages). v2 names cash paid in (counter, deposit machine, Post Office and PayPoint wording) as cash_deposit, and names cash-withdrawal wording that only the cash round-trip indicator reads. The ambiguity policy (R4) applies to credits under v2. Debits keep first-match order through the same stages, and v1 is untouched (R54). Each line records which rule set its category.
99:  - *The verdict on a credit*: collect every rule that matches. A match inside a longer match from another rule counts only as the longer one (STRIPE inside STRIPE CAPITAL, LOAN inside DIRECTOR LOAN, REFUND inside HMRC REFUND). A returned-payment code the bank prints at the start of the line settles the line type on its own, because it is the bank's code, not payer wording. Otherwise, if any cue matches, the cues decide: cues in one bucket give that bucket, and cues in two or more buckets, or a rail together with cash paid-in wording, make the credit ambiguous. With no cue, the names decide the same way. Matches in the same bucket agree, and the first in table order is the rule recorded.
101:  - *No hidden tie-breaks*: a rule's own exceptions may exclude wording that is on no list (a cheque is not cash), but never another list's cue or name. Where two lists' wording meets, either a longer phrase goes on one list, or the credit is ambiguous.
102:  - *Precedence*, highest first: (1) a per-line override; (2) a protected verdict, meaning the line-type cues plus lender, director, capital-introduced, shareholder and generic loan wording (R21's patterns and the cues); (3) a case-profile name (R5, R6) or an own-account pairing (R8); (4) an ambiguous verdict, which puts the credit in unexplained money in with every candidate recorded; (5) firm rules, which therefore never reach a line settled at (2) or (4) (R21, R4); (6) an unprotected verdict: the trading lists, invoice references, cash paid in and explained non-trading words; (7) the default, where a debit is supplier and a credit is unexplained.
103:  - Cash paid in is not protected (revision 2 never listed it), so a director named in the profile outranks cash wording, and a director's cash is director funding (R5). Every protected verdict, and every category a profile name or a pairing gives, is non-trading. So the only routes into trading receipts are an unambiguous trading verdict, a firm rule on a line that steps (2) to (4) did not settle, and a person's override.
107:- **C5 Review workspace** (new src/cma/review/). A loopback server and one bundled page with no build step. It renders what C2 and policy.evaluate computed and never computes money. The override form offers only categories valid for the line's direction. While show_policy_verdict is on, a policy-check panel shows the outcome and each rule's result on the current figures, with the illustrative label in the panel. For an ambiguous credit, the judgement queue and the drill-down show each rule it matched and the bucket that rule would give, so the underwriter's override starts from the candidates.
154:- A score, a model or a rule order that picks between the candidates for an ambiguous credit (Option E and the D52 sub-decision).
155:- Firm rules that settle an ambiguous credit.
166:Tier 1, demonstrating the mechanism (D23): R1-R7, R10-R19, R22-R31, R33-R35, R42, R43, R47, R48, R52-R56 (39 requirements), plus the stopwatch trial sheet (documentation only). It covers the v2 tables with real acquirers, lenders and cash wording and the two-stage verdict with the ambiguity policy, the buckets with cash inside trading receipts, the monthly summary and indicators, single-account cases with a profile and an override log, the workspace with the illustrative policy check, the preview, pack and sign-off, the offline lock, and a synthetic case shaped like a business.
195:- **Platform.** *Blast radius:* one Python package; new optional parts; the evidence JSON gains fields; PolicyResult and the checkpoint allow-list do not change. *Old and new side by side:* `cma run` is untouched, new commands are additive, and CMA_RULESET=v1 reproduces today's categories, cash included, with first-match order and no ambiguity verdict. *Resources:* files on disk only; no tables, queues, cron jobs or IAM. *Rollback:* an environment flag (CMA_RULESET), a config flip (show_policy_verdict) or a reinstall, with no data repair. *Observability:* see that section.
196:- **Staff.** *Smallest change:* Tier 1 is the smallest set that shows the mechanism in the reply's terms; Tier 2 can be cut off. The cash change is one category mapped into an existing set, and the policy check reuses policy.evaluate and the guard as they are. The ambiguity policy replaces per-pair exceptions with one verdict and keeps revision 2's precedence, inserting that verdict between the profile and firm rules. *Ownership:* arithmetic stays in metrics.py and indicators.py, the outcome in policy.py, categorisation in categorise.py plus the rule data; the workspace and pack only render. *Six-month misunderstandings removed:* M16 is renamed so it cannot be confused with M26 (unexplained); M18 is the cash inside M2, not a bucket beside it; [R1] to [R9] in a pack are policy rules, not requirement ids; the action reads 'sign off figures', not 'approve the loan'; OTHER means unexplained; an ambiguous credit is unexplained because the lists disagree, not because nothing matched, and its rule says conflict and names the candidates; pseudonymised data is still personal data. *Most likely wrong:* the memo fields (E8), the cash default (D31) and limits (D35), and the reading of D52. *Out of scope:* listed above.
214:| other | unexplained money in (M25), ambiguous credits included |
220:**Line provenance.** The rule field holds `<ruleset>:<rule id>`, `<ruleset>:default`, `<ruleset>:round_trip`, `profile:director`, `profile:own_account`, `firm:<rule id>`, or the override's reference. An ambiguous credit holds `<ruleset>:conflict:` followed by every candidate rule id in table order, joined by +, and its category is other (unexplained). The ruleset is always the text before the first colon.
222:**New figures.** No id is ever reused. M1-M16 keep their ids and units. M16 is renamed 'Non-trading money in (share)'; M2 keeps the name Total revenue, and its method sentence now names cash paid in. Values move only on lines a v2 rule now recognises or finds ambiguous (R10).
239:Reconciliation: M17 = M2 + M19 + M20 + M21 + M22 + M23 + M24 + M25, in pence. M18 is part of M2, is not added again, and its evidence is a subset of M2's. M16 and M26 share one denominator: money in less lending drawdowns, returned direct debits and own-account transfers. Cash deposits are trading receipts, so they are in neither numerator; an ambiguous credit is unexplained, so it is in both. Credits that fall to OTHER today keep the same treatment in M9 whichever new bucket they land in, with one exception: cash deposits now count as trading cash (D31). No committed fixture or synthetic case has a cash line (Consumer trace, item 13), so no committed value moves because of cash. M27 averages the closing balance of every calendar day in the period, carried over days with no lines, as M6 does. M28 adds the proposed monthly repayment for each month of the period to existing debt service.
243:**Indicators.** Each has an id, a kind, the threshold that fired, evidence ids and a one-line reason. Thresholds live in a packaged, validated YAML file that the firm can replace. Amounts in it are pounds with at most two decimals and are compared in pence. While a threshold still has its shipped value, the workspace and the pack call it illustrative, as they do the policy. Kinds: a large single credit against typical trading receipts; a cash deposit at or above the single-deposit limit; a month with cash above a set share of its trading receipts; cash paid in within a window of days after a cash withdrawal, the two amounts within a tolerance; a payment to a gambling operator; a returned direct debit; a run of consecutive overdrawn days; an irregular credit from a named acquirer. Shipped cash values: a single deposit of £2,500 or more and cash above 50% of a month's trading receipts (D35), and a round trip within 7 days and 10% of the amount (design default). An ambiguous credit is not categorised as cash, so the cash indicators do not read it; it is in the judgement queue as unexplained.
289:**Review workspace API.** Internal to the package; the page and the server ship together, so it is not a shared contract. It can read the case summary; read the lines behind a figure, bucket, monthly cell or indicator; read the policy check when it is shown; post an override with the expected log position; preview and promote a firm rule; build a preview; sign off a preview's snapshot; and post a comparison figure. A line in the judgement queue or a drill-down carries its rule, and for an ambiguous credit its candidate rules and the bucket each would give. The firm-rule preview counts the lines the rule matches and will not apply to, because a protected or ambiguous verdict settled them (R20, R21, R4). Every request carries the session token. Every write carries the expected log position and gets a conflict if that position is stale.
321:| A director named in the profile pays in cash, or sends a credit whose wording is ambiguous | The profile name settles it: director funding | R5, R4 |
322:| A firm rule matches an ambiguous credit, or one a protected verdict settled | The firm rule is not applied; the preview counts such lines before saving | R4, R20, R21 |
323:| The underwriter moves an ambiguous credit into trading receipts | Recorded as an override with a reason; the figures recompute and mark the line as overridden | R17, R4 |
359:- Committed fixtures (revision 3): the change that lands the verdict lists every committed line whose v2 category differs from v1, ambiguous credits with their candidates among them (R10, P7). The synthetic generator's existing credits carry wording from one list at most: FPS CREDIT DIRECTOR CAPITAL INTRODUCED has a rail and director wording only, so it stays director funding (src/cma/synth.py:225).
362:- Reversible: an older package ignores case folders it does not know and reads the v1 evidence keys it knows. CMA_RULESET=v1 brings back the old categorisation, with cash falling to OTHER and no ambiguity verdict, without a reinstall.
374:2. Land the v2 tables (cash wording included) and the two-stage verdict with the ambiguity policy, then the buckets, monthly summary and indicators, behind CMA_RULESET. Make v2 the default when evals, the pairwise matrix, the rule-order shuffle and truth for the new synthetic features pass, the cash truth included.
380:**Rollback.** For the policy check, set show_policy_verdict off. For categorisation, cash and the ambiguity verdict included, set CMA_RULESET=v1 or reinstall the previous wheel. Packs and evidence are immutable and record their ruleset, thresholds, policy hash, the show_policy_verdict setting and package version, so nothing needs repair. The old version leaves case folders written by the new one alone.
388:- **The signal that tests the ambiguity policy (D52):** per case, the number of ambiguous credits, their share of money in, and the candidate pairs, read from line provenance in each pack's evidence and counted among the judgement-queue lines in `cma report`. A pair that keeps recurring, and that the underwriter keeps moving the same way, needs a longer phrase on one list, not a rule order.
391:- **Log lines:** one structured line per event in the case log, with the event, case id, snapshot and counts, and no names or descriptions. For example: event=pack_written traced=100 reconciled=true unexplained_pct=12.4 ambiguous=4 cash_flagged=3 policy_check=shown.
398:- **Trust boundary:** bank data the applicant controls enters through the importers, and the loader checks its structure and balance chain. Descriptions are written by payers: they are escaped in HTML under a strict content security policy with no inline script, formula characters are neutralised in CSV, and they never go into prompts. Candidate rule ids shown for an ambiguous credit come from the shipped tables, not from payer text.
412:4. **M16's name and its share denominator.** `rg -n -i 'unclassified' -g '!.bridgie/**'`; `rg -n 'unexplained' src/cma/policies`. Consumers: src/cma/metrics.py:289, src/cma/llm.py:56, src/cma/categorise.py:12 (docstring), src/cma/synth.py (truth field), src/cma/policies/uk_sme_default.yaml:86 (R9 description), README.md:69,118,124, evals/run_evals.py:100, tests/test_metrics.py:53, tests/test_import_monzo.py:63-65, tests/test_panel_run02_metrics.py:73, tests/test_panel_run03.py:130, examples/cafe_stretched.memo.md:5,44,56, examples/cafe_stretched.memo.evidence.json, examples/cafe_stretched.truth.json. Effect: rename and wording only. Values change only where own-account lines now leave the denominator, cash lines leave the numerator, or ambiguous credits stay in it; those fixtures are listed in the change, and today none has a cash line.
   345	    return bool(names) and re.search(names_pattern(names), description) is not None
   346	
   347	
   348	def _spans(rule: Rule, description: str) -> list[tuple[int, int]]:
   349	    return [m.span() for m in rule.pattern.finditer(description) if m.end() > m.start()]
   350	
   351	
   352	def credit_matches(ruleset: Ruleset, description: str, amount: float) -> list[Rule]:
   353	    """Every rule of `ruleset` that matches a line, in table order, less those whose every
   354	    match lies inside a longer match of another rule (STRIPE inside STRIPE CAPITAL, REFUND
   355	    inside HMRC REFUND, LOAN inside DIRECTOR LOAN): those count only as the longer one."""
   356	    desc = description.upper()
   357	    matched = [(r, _spans(r, desc)) for r in ruleset.rules if rule_matches(r, desc, amount)]
   358	
   359	    def inside_longer(rule: Rule, spans: list[tuple[int, int]]) -> bool:
   360	        others = [o for other, ss in matched if other is not rule for o in ss]
   361	        return bool(spans) and all(any(a <= s and e <= b and b - a > e - s for a, b in others) for s, e in spans)
   362	
   363	    return [r for r, spans in matched if not inside_longer(r, spans)]
   364	
   365	
   366	@dataclass(frozen=True)
   367	class Verdict:
   368	    """What the table says about a credit: the rule that settles it, or, when the wording
   369	    points to more than one money-in bucket, every candidate rule."""
   370	
   371	    rules: tuple[Rule, ...]
   372	    ambiguous: bool = False
   373	
   374	    @property
   375	    def rule(self) -> Rule:
   376	        return self.rules[0]
   377	
   378	
   379	def _bucket(rule: Rule) -> object:
   380	    return CREDIT_BUCKET.get(rule.category, rule.category) if rule.category is not None else None
   381	
   382	
   383	def _decide(rules: Sequence[Rule]) -> Verdict:
   384	    if len({_bucket(r) for r in rules}) > 1:
   385	        return Verdict(tuple(rules), ambiguous=True)
   386	    return Verdict((rules[0],))
   387	
   388	
   389	def credit_verdict(ruleset: Ruleset, description: str, amount: float) -> Verdict | None:
   390	    """R4's verdict on a credit under a table that is not frozen, or None when no rule
   391	    with a category matches."""
   392	    rules = credit_matches(ruleset, description, amount)
   393	    cues = [r for r in rules if r.stage == "line_type"]
   394	    bank = [r for r in cues if r.category is not None and r.pattern.pattern.startswith("^")]
   395	    if bank:  # the bank's own code at the start of the line (UNPAID DD), not payer wording
   396	        return Verdict((bank[0],))
   397	    bucketed = [r for r in cues if r.category is not None]
   398	    if bucketed:
   399	        return _decide(bucketed)
   400	    names = [r for r in rules if r.stage == "names" and r.category is not None]
   401	    rails = [r for r in cues if r.category is None]
   402	    if rails and any(r.category is Category.CASH_DEPOSIT for r in names):
   403	        return Verdict(tuple(rails + names), ambiguous=True)  # electronic money is not cash
   404	    return _decide(names) if names else None
   405	
   406	
   407	def classify(
   408	    t: Transaction,
   409	    ruleset: Ruleset | None = None,
   410	    *,
   411	    override: LineOverride | None = None,
   412	    profile: ProfileNames | None = None,
   413	    firm_rules: Sequence[Rule] = (),
   414	) -> tuple[Category, str]:
   415	    """The category of one line and the id of the rule that set it, in precedence order."""
   416	    ruleset = ruleset or active_ruleset()
   417	    if override is not None:
   418	        return override.category, override.ref
   419	    desc = t.description.upper()
   420	    if t.amount > 0 and not ruleset.frozen:
   421	        return _classify_credit(t, desc, ruleset, profile, firm_rules)
   422	    for rule in ruleset.protected:
   423	        if rule.category is not None and rule_matches(rule, desc, t.amount):
   424	            return rule.category, f"{ruleset.id}:{rule.id}"
   425	    found = _profile(t, desc, profile)
   426	    if found:
   427	        return found
   428	    for rule in firm_rules:
   429	        if rule_matches(rule, desc, t.amount):
   430	            return rule.category, f"firm:{rule.id}"
   431	    for rule in ruleset.unprotected:
   432	        if rule.category is not None and rule_matches(rule, desc, t.amount):
   433	            return rule.category, f"{ruleset.id}:{rule.id}"
   434	    return (Category.SUPPLIER if t.amount < 0 else Category.OTHER), f"{ruleset.id}:default"
   435	
   436	
   437	def _profile(t: Transaction, desc: str, profile: ProfileNames | None) -> tuple[Category, str] | None:
   438	    if profile is not None:
   439	        if t.amount > 0 and _names_in(profile.directors, desc):
   440	            return Category.DIRECTOR_FUNDING, "profile:director"
   441	        if t.amount != 0 and _names_in(profile.own_accounts, desc):
   442	            return Category.OWN_ACCOUNT_TRANSFER, "profile:own_account"
   443	    return None
   444	
   445	
   446	def _classify_credit(t: Transaction, desc: str, ruleset: Ruleset, profile: ProfileNames | None,
   447	                     firm_rules: Sequence[Rule]) -> tuple[Category, str]:
   448	    """Design C1's precedence for a credit, after the override: (2) a protected verdict,
   449	    (3) a profile name, (4) an ambiguous verdict, (5) firm rules, (6) an unprotected
   450	    verdict, (7) the default. Only (3) and an override settle an ambiguous credit."""
   451	    verdict = credit_verdict(ruleset, desc, t.amount)
   452	    if verdict is not None and not verdict.ambiguous and verdict.rule.protected:
   453	        return verdict.rule.category, f"{ruleset.id}:{verdict.rule.id}"
   454	    found = _profile(t, desc, profile)
   455	    if found:
   456	        return found
   457	    if verdict is not None and verdict.ambiguous:
   458	        return Category.OTHER, f"{ruleset.id}:conflict:" + "+".join(r.id for r in verdict.rules)
   459	    for rule in firm_rules:
   460	        if rule_matches(rule, desc, t.amount):
   461	            return rule.category, f"firm:{rule.id}"
   462	    if verdict is not None:
   463	        return verdict.rule.category, f"{ruleset.id}:{verdict.rule.id}"
   464	    return Category.OTHER, f"{ruleset.id}:default"
   465	
